 ## Subtopics
 
+### [GPU-Driven Tetrahedral Mesh Rasterization Pipeline](7923-gpu-driven-tetrahedral-mesh-rasterization-pipeline.md)
+This method manages the rendering of tetrahedral meshes on the GPU by handling sorting algorithms, buffer creation for triangles and tetrahedra, and indirect draw/dispatch command setup. It serves as the execution engine for the forward intersect raster pass, projecting tetrahedral mesh cells into triangles, sorting them back-to-front, and rasterizing them with back-to-front blending to compute volume color and opacity. It builds on [Two-Pass GPU Approach](1339-two-pass-gpu-approach.md) by replacing fixed pipeline stages with a fully GPU-driven indirect dispatch mechanism that adapts to the variable number of triangles generated per tetrahedron.
+
 ### Pure Emissive Rendering
-A variant that simply adds the contribution of each tetrahedron without complex compositing [S5]. It uses a 2D texture map containing the integral of the transfer function for the interval $[\min(s_f, s_b), \max(s_f, s_b)]$ at position $(s_f, s_b)$ [S5]. The dependency of color on ray segment length is linear, allowing integration by modulating the shading lookup with the minimum texture lookup using register combiners [S5].
+A rendering technique that simply adds the contribution of each tetrahedron [S5]. It uses a 2D texture map since the dependency of the color contribution on the length of the ray segment is linear and can be integrated by modulating the result of the shading lookup with the result of the minimum texture lookup [S5]. It uses the register combiners extension to multiply the result of texture stage 2 with the r coordinate of texture stage 0 [S5]. The texture map in stage 2 contains the integral of the transfer function for the interval $[\min(s_f, s_b), \max(s_f, s_b)]$ at position $(s_f, s_b)$ [S5].
 
 ### Rendering Algorithm (2nd Order)
-A variant that projects front and back facing quadrilaterals of the bounding box to two distinct 32-bit floating point auxiliary buffers using a fragment shader that writes interpolated 3D texture coordinates [S6]. It uses the `GL_ARB_draw_buffers` extension for multiple render targets [S6].
+A variant that projects front and back facing quadrilaterals of the bounding box to two distinct 32-bit floating-point auxiliary buffers using a fragment shader that writes interpolated 3D texture coordinates [S6]. It uses the OpenGL extension `GL_ARB_draw_buffers` for rendering multiple targets [S6].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** GPU-bound; CPU is under-utilized [S2]. Performance dips slightly when the isosurface level changes due to pre-pass re-culling overhead [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** High. Pre-computation requires 160 bytes per tetrahedron [S1, unofficial]. Model size is restricted to 600,000 tetrahedra on an ATI Radeon 9700 (128 MB RAM) due to memory constraints [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Achieves interactive frame rates up to 2.0 M Tets/s on current graphics hardware [S3]. Mid-sized models achieve 2 to 5 fps [S1, unofficial]. Volumes up to $64^3$ run in interactive rates after optimization; larger volumes require multiple passes [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Interactive frame rates, supports interactive transfer function editing and isosurface highlighting with Phong shading [S3][S4, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High memory consumption; transfer function updates (changing opacity levels) require recreating arrays, reducing interactivity but increasing FPS [S4, unofficial]. Convolution of a $128^3$ volume with a $6^3$ filter takes >30 seconds [S2].
-*   **When to use:** When interactive visualization of tetrahedral meshes is required on consumer hardware and memory allows for the pre-computed data structures [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** For very large volumes ($>64^3$) without multi-pass strategies, or when memory is severely constrained [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Interactive frame rates up to 2.0 M Tets/s on current graphics hardware [S3]. Mid-sized models achieve 2 to 5 fps [S1, unofficial]. Volumes up to $64^3$ achieve interactive frame rates after optimization; larger volumes require multiple passes [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** 160 bytes per tetrahedron due to pre-computation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** GPU bound and CPU under-utilized [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Efficient ray-casting for tetrahedral meshes on consumer hardware; supports interactive transfer function editing and isosurface highlighting; reduces CPU-GPU data transfer [S3].
+*   **Limitations:** Model size restricted by GPU memory (e.g., 600k tets on older cards); performance dips when isosurface level changes; transfer function updates require array recreation [S1, unofficial][S2][S4, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Interactive visualization of unstructured tetrahedral volumes where hardware acceleration is available and memory constraints allow for the pre-computation overhead.
+*   **When not to use:** Extremely large models exceeding GPU memory limits without paging; scenarios requiring real-time isosurface changes with minimal latency.
 
 ## Background
-The method was developed by Weiler et al. as an efficient ray-caster for tetrahedral meshes on consumer graphics cards [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the Projected Tetrahedra algorithm and incorporates techniques from BT Volume rendering [S2]. The approach was presented in papers around 2007-2008 [S3][S4, unofficial].
+The method was introduced by Weiler et al. as an efficient ray-caster for tetrahedral meshes implemented on consumer graphics cards [S1, S4, unofficial]. It was developed to address the limitations of CPU-based ray casting and the high memory costs of earlier GPU approaches [S3]. The approach leverages fragment programs and pre-integration techniques [S1, S4, unofficial]. It is used in the context of BT Volumes (Bézier Tetrahedra Volumes) for isosurface and volume rendering [S2].
 
